{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-10","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"10","title":"A joint resolution terminating the national emergency declared with respect to energy.","introducedDate":"2025-02-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-26","latestActionText":"Failed of passage in Senate by Yea-Nay Vote. 47 - 52. Record Vote Number: 95. (consideration: CR S1364, S1367-1390)","policyArea":"Emergency Management","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Alternative and renewable resources","Building construction","Coal","Energy storage, supplies, demand","Licensing and registrations","Mining","Oil and gas"],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000384","cosponsorBioguideIds":["H001046","S001194","M001176","H001042","S000033","R000122","M000133","V000128","W000779","B001288","S001181"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution terminates the national emergency relating to energy declared by the President on January 20, 2025, in Executive Order 14156. The executive order states that the supply of and infrastructure for energy in the United States is insufficient to meet the country's needs. It defines energy as crude oil, natural gas, lease condensates, natural gas liquids, refined petroleum products, uranium, coal, biofuels, geothermal heat, the kinetic movement of flowing water, and critical minerals. The executive order directs the heads of executive departments and agencies to use available emergency and other authorities to take certain actions to address this topic, including approving development of domestic energy resources, expediting the completion of authorized energy infrastructure (particularly in the Northeast, West Coast, and Alaska), and pursuing the use of emergency permitting provisions under certain environmental regulations. The executive order also directs the Department of Defense to conduct an assessment of its ability to acquire and transport energy resources (particularly in the Northeast and West Coast), and invokes emergency military construction authority to address any vulnerabilities identified in the assessment.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/10","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres10.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:30.705Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-10","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would end the national energy emergency that the President declared in January 2025. That emergency declaration directed federal agencies to speed up approvals for energy projects—including oil, gas, coal, renewables, and infrastructure like pipelines—and gave the Defense Department authority to use emergency construction powers to address energy supply vulnerabilities. Terminating the emergency would remove those expedited authorities and return permitting and development processes to their standard procedures.","industries":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The emergency declaration explicitly directs expedited approval of oil, gas, and coal development and infrastructure; terminating it would remove those fast-track authorities."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The emergency order covers renewable resources and energy storage; ending it would affect expedited permitting for solar, wind, and grid projects."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The emergency declaration includes critical minerals and uranium extraction; termination would remove expedited development authorities for these materials."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The emergency order addresses energy infrastructure and supply adequacy; utilities would lose access to expedited permitting for transmission and generation projects."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The emergency invokes military construction authority and expedited infrastructure completion; terminating it would affect contractors working on energy-related projects."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"f89dbd42b877b6a9c636b942","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:52:35.405Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would end the national emergency relating to energy declared by the President on January 20, 2025, in Executive Order 14156.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys fuel or heats a home. People who live near a mine, and people who work in one.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what fuel costs. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-sjres-10","bioguideId":"H001046","cycle":2026,"score":0.017983378004122873,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":69500,"donorShare":0.0720884250105345,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.013106986365551725},{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":8500,"donorShare":0.00881656996531717,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0021755172641691714},{"industry":"energy-renewable","donorAmount":6500,"donorShare":0.006742082914654306,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0014885118123262753},{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.0077793264399857375,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0012123625620756994}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6943113675407604},"member":{"name":"Martin Heinrich","chamber":"Senate","state":"NM","imageUrl":"data/portraits/H001046.jpg","role":"Cos